# How much of the system this corpus claims to hold. One row per extent a stack declares, counted
# over the documents that attest to a world.
#
# The coupling search, one level up
#   The same shape, and the same trap. `scoped` says somebody established what lies outside this
#   stack and excluded it on purpose. `unbounded` says nobody looked. Rendered as one boolean, "is
#   this stack complete?", a bounded selection and an unexamined one are the same row, and the
#   difference is gone for good. It is the coupling search's distinction applied to the stack
#   itself.
#
# The answer nobody gives
#   The answer nobody in this corpus gives is the one worth naming: no filing claims `complete`.
#   A stack is a projection of one system, held by whoever filed it, and reading it as an
#   enumeration of the system is what turns a second document's extra layer into an accusation
#   that the first omitted something. The corpus declining to claim completeness is the model's
#   own reading, filed rather than asserted.
#
# A report, not a rule
#   `complete` is falsifiable in principle and by nothing here, which is why this is a report and
#   not a rule. What would refute it is another filing holding a layer of the same system that
#   this one lacks, and no document names a system, only a filing.
-- pm:Stack/pm:scope over scope/corpus.sqlc; the extent axis is documented on that element.
SELECT CASE
         WHEN sc.extent = 'complete'  THEN 'the whole system is in this stack'
         WHEN sc.extent = 'scoped'    THEN 'a bounded selection: somebody said what is outside'
         WHEN sc.extent = 'unbounded' THEN 'NOBODY LOOKED at what lies outside'
         ELSE 'not stated: ' || sc.absent
       END      AS how_much_of_the_system,
       count(*) AS stacks
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/scopes.sqlc)
) sc
JOIN      (
    :compose(scope/corpus.sqlc)
) s USING (filing)
GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
